A group is one character, scene or prop under one name. Combine complementary details that describe the same appearance. Keep visually incompatible appearances as separate forms of that one element: never combine different outfits, hairstyles, ages, weather, lighting, damage or other visible states into one description, and never discard one merely because another reading disagrees. The earliest appearance is the top-level default description; every other distinct appearance is an entry in forms with a short visible-feature name rather than a time label. Reconcile identical forms under one name and keep genuinely different forms separate. Use an empty forms list when there is only one appearance.
Every default and form description must be fully self-contained, static, precise and unambiguous, repeating all shared details rather than referring to another form or saying "same as before". Never write alternatives or temporal changes into a description: no "或", "或者", "or", "either", "前期/后期", "early/late", "before/after", conditions, or choices of clothing, age, hairstyle, light or state. For a character, state apparent age, build, facial features and marks, hair colour, length and style, upper garment, lower garment, socks and footwear, and every accessory and item worn or carried, with exact colours, materials, shapes and details; say explicitly when a category has none. For a scene, give exact layout, size, fixed furnishings, materials, light and one static state. For a prop, give exact shape, dimensions, colour, material, markings and wear in one static state. Include only visible appearance, never voice, habits, manner, relationships, backstory or events.
When a visible detail is missing, settle it as one concrete design consistent with all the readings and reuse that exact detail in every form where it applies. Do not offer alternatives, invent new people, elements, events or conflicting facts, rename the element, or add or leave out an entry: answer for exactly the names above, one each.
